 enough  single cell suspension such that you  know they are all  separated and cells don't stick to each  other  after this we are going to flow this  into a microfluidic device  with a dna barcode beat that i had uh  talked to you earlier  we are going to flow them in such a  fashion that there  is one cell and one barcode bead  in a given droplet this is mainly done  by adjusting the concentration of the  cells and the  barcode bead once the cells are in the  uh are encapsulated  into there is also a lysis agent that  was flown in with the  uh dna barcode beads that is going to  destabilize the cell membrane and have  the rna released into the droplet  and what is going to happen is this rna  because of the high concentration of  molecules  in a droplet it's going to find dna  barcodes that are attached to the beads  and come eyes to the poly t section that  i had shown you before  so the end result is we are going to  sample  the mrna from a given cell onto  uh using these dna barcode beads um  such that a fraction of the mrna is  uh hybridized onto the uh onto the bead  once this happens we can then break the  droplets we can then perform reverse  transcription  this can be done both inside the  droplets or also the droplets  and there are uh different techniques  that do  either or once the reverse transcription  happens  the rna gets converted into stable  cdna which are complementary dna that  has the barcode the dna barcode attached  to it  we then collect all this dna barcode and  uh dna barcoded dna and uh  you amp and make library  sequence for most of the cases uh  illumina based sequencing  and uh that that is a  or a a standard that is  uh practiced in most cases  so here we have a dropseek  device setup that is run by syringe  pumps  there is the device sitting uh the  droplets are getting generated  in the microfluidic devices where the  speeds and lysis  agents meet those are getting and  captain these droplets with the beads  and then they  flow o